<section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="prose fade-in">
            <h2>The situation</h2>
            <p>Southern Communications Group's business customers manage their phone lines, mobiles and broadband through a self-service portal: usage alerts, orders, invoices, access for their own staff, and service requests such as ceasing a line or swapping a SIM.</p>
            <p>The portal had been built on Microsoft Power Pages over SCG's Dynamics 365 platform. It had grown to 260 web pages, 37 forms and 69 lists, with custom JavaScript on 23 pages, and around 14,000 people sign in to it across more than 12,000 customer accounts.</p>
            <p>Elephas has worked with SCG since 2018 — first with 2 Circles, before it became part of the group — including the quote-to-order apps its sales teams use alongside Dynamics 365.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="container">
        <h2 class="fade-in">What we did</h2>
        <ul class="checklist">
            <li class="fade-in">Kept Dynamics 365 as the system of record: the portal stores no customer data of its own</li>
            <li class="fade-in">Built a configurable grid and form engine, so pages, forms and Dataverse queries are set up in the CMS rather than hard-coded</li>
            <li class="fade-in">Encrypted query and form definitions on the server, so the browser cannot change which records are read or written</li>
            <li class="fade-in">Wrote tooling that reads the live Power Pages configuration from Dataverse and compares it with the new portal, so parity is checked rather than assumed</li>
            <li class="fade-in">Covered the portal with 575 unit tests and Playwright browser tests</li>
            <li class="fade-in">Planned a cutover where rollback is a DNS change, because both portals share the same Dataverse</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

